 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method is computationally expensive due to skeleton computation (potential field method is the slowest examined technique) and bounding box/convexity calculations [S3]. Preprocessing time is not a main issue for the use case [S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the volume, binary mask, distance map, and skeleton data structures.
-*   **Strengths:** Allows visual separation of objects with similar intensities but different shapes (e.g., contrast-filled heart vs. vessels) without complex segmentation [S3]. The notion of shape is intuitive for domain experts [S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires proper masking/pre-segmentation, which can be challenging [S1, unofficial]. Imperfect pre-segmentations cause instabilities in shape-skeletons [S3]. Tubiness classifier reliability depends on skeleton quality; incomplete skeletons boost standard deviation [S3]. Heavily branching skeletons cause problems for decomposition and merging [S3].
-*   **When to use:** When features have overlapping intensity ranges and distinct 3D shapes (tubular, planar, blobby).
-*   **When not to use:** When pre-segmentation is impossible or highly unreliable, or when real-time performance is critical and preprocessing time is prohibitive.
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   Skeleton computation using the potential field method is the slowest of examined techniques, but preprocessing time is not a main issue for the use case [S3].
+*   Memory usage depends on storing the distance map and skeleton segments [S3].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   Tested on data sets of sizes $256^3$ to $512^3$ [S3].
+*   Successfully distinguishes blobby heart structures filled with contrast agents from occluding vessels and rib bones [S3].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Allows visual separation of objects with similar intensity but different shapes; more intuitive user interface than per-voxel methods; robust to moderate within-class deformation [S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires proper masking/pre-segmentation, which can be challenging; imperfect pre-segmentations cause instabilities in shape-skeletons; tubiness classifier reliability depends on skeleton quality (incomplete segments boost standard deviation); cavities inside pre-segmentation disturb skeleton computation [S3].
+
+**When to Use:**
+*   When features have overlapping intensity ranges but distinct shapes (e.g., medical imaging with contrast agents) [S3].
+*   When an intuitive, feature-based classification is preferred over per-voxel histogram interpretation [S3].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+*   When high-quality manual segmentation is not feasible and windowing is insufficient [S3].
+*   When real-time performance is critical and potential field skeletonization is too slow [S3].
 
